# Brief — Snake (round 1)

Owner: `producer`. Source: the `Game objective` issue (`producer`#1). No other material was supplied.

## What the input asks (quoted)

> Make Snake as a web game played in the browser: the snake moves on a grid and is turned with the arrow keys, eating food makes it one cell longer and adds a point, and running into a wall or itself ends the game. It has a title screen, the score during play, and a game-over screen with the final score and a way to play again.

## Requirements, as the acceptance standard

Each is judged by playing the assembled delivery in a desktop browser (Chromium), opened from `index.html` with no build step or server-side code required.

| # | Requirement (from the input) | Judged by |
|---|---|---|
| R1 | Web game played in the browser | `index.html` at the delivery root opens and runs in Chromium with no console errors; works from a static file server and needs no network at play time. |
| R2 | The snake moves on a grid | Visible grid/board; the snake advances one cell at a time at a steady, playable pace, continuously, in its current direction. |
| R3 | Turned with the arrow keys | Up/Down/Left/Right change direction on the next step; reversing straight into itself (180°) is not taken as a turn that kills the snake; two quick key presses within one step are not lost or turned into a self-kill. Arrow keys do not scroll the page. |
| R4 | Eating food makes it one cell longer and adds a point | Exactly +1 length and +1 score per food; new food appears on a free cell (never on the snake). |
| R5 | Running into a wall ends the game | Moving out of the board on any of the four sides ends the game (no wrap-around). |
| R6 | Running into itself ends the game | Head entering a body cell ends the game. |
| R7 | Title screen | Shown on load, names the game, says how to start and how to control; play starts only from it. |
| R8 | Score during play | Current score visible and updating while playing. |
| R9 | Game-over screen with the final score | Shown when the game ends; shows the final score of that run. |
| R10 | A way to play again | From game over, a clear key and/or button starts a fresh run (score 0, initial length, new food) without reloading the page. |

## Focus and priorities for this round

1. **Correct core rules first** (R2–R6): movement, turning, growth, scoring and both death conditions must be right and robust; this is what the game is.
2. **The full loop of screens** (R7–R10): title → play → game over → play again, all reachable by keyboard.
3. **Readable, consistent presentation**: a coherent look (board, snake, food, screens, score) from the art position; sound is welcome but optional and must not be required to play.
4. Nothing beyond the input is required (no levels, leaderboards, mobile controls). Extras are fine only if they do not risk the above.

## Who does what (see `assignment.json`)

- `game-developer`: the game itself (code, behavior, build) — the delivery root.
- `artist`: the visual (and any audio) assets and their consistency, delivered under `assets/`, in formats and sizes agreed with `game-developer`.
- `qa`: systematic checks of each build against R1–R10, with reproducible defect reports filed to the owner.
- `playtester`: plays each build as a player and reports the experience with evidence (captures).
- `producer`: this brief, the assignment, and acceptance/approval.

## What could not be established

- No target grid size, speed, art style or platform beyond "the browser" is given; these are left to the owners, judged only by the standards above (playable, readable).
- Whether speed should increase with score is not specified; not required.
